            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Russian for a seashell, then the Russian for to dive."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: прогноз",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "прогноз (prognóz) — \"a forecast\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "По прогнозу завтра будет дождь. Po prognózu závtra búdet dozhd. According to the forecast it will rain tomorrow."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Masculine. Прогноз погоды — the weather forecast."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "прогноз (prognóz) is usually traced through Latin prognōsis to Greek prógnōsis 'knowing beforehand' (EtymDB lists both; the Russian entry has no source), from pro- 'before' and gnōsis 'knowledge'."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
